WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.541 --> 00:00:03.250
(intense music)
(water splashing)

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.610
- [Narrator] This can be
scary, and rightfully so.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.560
Sharks have patrolled the waters
for over 400 million years.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.680
And while they are powerful creatures,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.130
our stories have given them the reputation

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.903
of being vengeful killers.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.500
Hollywood says so.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.020
- You're gonna need a bigger boat.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.060
- [Narrator] So does the media.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.360
- [Reporter] A life or death struggle

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.220
with one of the ocean's
most dangerous predators.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.730
- [Narrator] But are sharks
really the human hunters?

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.330
- [Announcer] Terror of all
men who enter the ocean.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.323
- [Narrator] Or the hunted?

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:37.643
- [Reporter] Well, it's going to be

00:00:37.643 --> 00:00:38.880
a beautiful sunny day today.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.580
You might want to do some
of those outdoor activities.

00:00:43.295 --> 00:00:44.540
(upbeat surf music)

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.040
- I think the reason
that sharks are feared

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.342
by the general public is
pretty self-explanatory.

00:00:49.342 --> 00:00:50.175
(whistle tweeting)

00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:52.210
They frickin' bite people sometimes.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:53.660
- [Narrator] This is Jess Cramp,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.180
Nat Geo explorer and
founder of Sharks Pacific.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.350
She also lives near a lot of roosters,

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.855
so you might hear this.

00:01:00.855 --> 00:01:02.120
(rooster crowing)

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.150
But back to the shark bites.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.260
- It's unfortunate that it
happens, but it does happen,

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:07.590
so trying to avoid that fact,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.690
I feel like is a bit futile at this stage.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.670
- [Narrator] These rare
bites typically stem

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.750
from curiosity or mistaken identity,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.130
yet how we feel about
sharks versus the data,

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.513
that's a different story.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.083
Like a really different story.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.870
We think all sharks look like this,

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:28.780
and do this.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.613
But actually.

00:01:29.613 --> 00:01:32.240
- Sharks are an incredibly
diverse group of fish.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.070
Some sharks live most of their lives

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.290
in a really small radius,

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:36.570
just within a few coral heads,

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.050
and others will swim around the planet.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.260
They range from whale sharks,

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.650
which are the largest shark species,

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.480
and they get to be about 40 feet long,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.880
down to the dwarf lantern shark,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.150
which is about eight inches.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.830
- [Narrator] That's cool,

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.580
but when did they become the bad guys?

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.890
Let's ask one of the people

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.830
behind the International Shark
Attack File, George Burgess.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.610
- [George] As long as
humans entered the sea

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.730
or floated upon it,
the inevitable conflict

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.340
between humans and sharks was present.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.220
- [Narrator] From the Greeks,
Romans, and Spanish explorers,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.300
tales were written about
battles with sharks,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.290
but fast forward to when
leisure time became a thing,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.393
and Americans started going to beaches.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.370
The shark attacks from
those ancient tales,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.963
they weren't really happening.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.700
- [George] It became obvious

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.460
that shark attack
certainly wasn't as common

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.160
as people were saying it was.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.980
So the pendulum had swung
from one side to the other.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.300
- [Narrator] And this
millionaire was so convinced

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:42.300
shark attacks weren't a thing,
he offered a $500 reward

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.890
to anyone who could prove
him wrong, wild dude.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.750
As in, would jump into the water

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:51.330
with a real shark at parties wild, yeah.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.993
Also, many scientists at the time agreed.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.370
Until this happened.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.180
- [George] When 1916 came around,

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.070
and there was a series of attacks

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.250
that occurred on the New Jersey coastline,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.936
suddenly the real world slapped
everybody across the face.

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.340
(slapping)

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.880
- [Narrator] Starting on July 1st

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.840
and spanning two weeks' time,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.540
the coastline of New Jersey

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.510
witnessed five shark attacks
resulting in four deaths.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:18.940
People were spooked.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.170
Locals even attempted to fight back

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.980
by throwing dynamite into
the water, seriously.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.170
(upbeat jazzy music)

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.000
- [George] Because the
New Jersey coastline

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.970
was a popular vacation
place for the elite,

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.500
including the president,
there was immediate concern

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.790
and action that we had
to get this taken care of

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.920
because I got a vacation already
planned later this summer.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.110
- [Narrator] The White House
agreed to give federal aid

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:46.060
to "drive away all the
ferocious man-eating sharks

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.317
which have been making prey of bathers."

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.050
Eventually they put the blame

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.830
on an eight-foot juvenile great white,

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.630
but by then it was front page news.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.793
The damage was done.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.600
While 1916 played its role
on sharks' reputation,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.253
it was a fictional story
that really did a number.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.450
Yup. You guessed it.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.930
- [George] The book and the movie "Jaws,"

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.320
as in any good cinema or any good book,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.250
if you can get the reader,
the viewer fully immersed,

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:18.710
then you've done your job.

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:21.180
(dramatic music)

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.720
- [Narrator] Consider
the audience immersed.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.330
The novel by Peter Benchley
spent 44 weeks as a bestseller,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.920
and the movie became the largest
grossing film at the time.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.860
It essentially created
the summer blockbuster.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.220
- [George] People believed
every last bit of that was fact,

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.450
even though it took great
liberties with facts,

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.940
some knowingly, and some
just because we know more now

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.090
than what we knew then.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.510
- [Narrator] One such belief

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:52.420
was that sharks are
vengeful human hunters,

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.330
which in turn led to a surge

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.260
in humans hunting them.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.540
- [George] Shortly after
the movie came out,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.120
there was a great influx in
shark fishing tournaments

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.530
up and down the East coast
of the United States.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.373
As a result, shark
population began to decline.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.250
- [Narrator] Today, scientists estimate

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:13.040
a staggering 100 million
sharks are killed every year

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.510
around the world.

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:17.670
And to make matters worse,
sharks are specially vulnerable

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.720
because they take longer to mature

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.463
and typically produce their
young at a slower rate.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:25.990
- So sharks are in trouble.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.510
While habitat destruction,
pollution, and climate change

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.940
are definitely problems for sharks,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.170
our biggest impact is through fisheries.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.760
- [Narrator] Overfishing of
sharks is predominantly led

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.300
by the demand for their
fins, meat, and liver oil,

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.750
and the lack of management

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:46.020
to ensure shark fisheries are sustainable.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.460
- The way that we can make
the most impact for sharks

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.980
is actually through working
with fishing agencies,

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.720
with fishermen, also spatial protections

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.620
on important critical habitat for them

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.790
to try to reduce mortality and
create a few more safe havens

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.190
where they can actually
hang out and repopulate.

00:06:03.735 --> 00:06:05.420
(soft marimba music)

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.270
- [Narrator] The common
narrative of sharks

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:11.830
will likely continue to be on
rare attacks, but in reality,

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.940
the species are in need.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.490
Even Peter Benchley came to this truth

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:20.030
as he later focused his
efforts on shark conservation.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.000
- [Peter] If there's one
thing I know for dead certain,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.060
it's that I couldn't
possibly write "Jaws" today.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.853
I could not turn this
beautiful beast into a villain.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.760
- Shark conservation is
not a one size fits all.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.880
It's a multipronged problem,
but they are fixable,

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.610
and that's the one thing that
I think is really important

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:39.443
for people to know

00:06:39.443 --> 00:06:41.010
is that these are problems

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:44.543
that while we may have created
them, they are fixable.

